]\n33:T5fc,The main force behind contemporary Martian geology is its atmosphere, which is primarily composed of carbon dioxide, nitrogen, and argon. By Earth measures, the air is absurdly thin; air pressure at the summit of Mount Everest is roughly 50 times greater than it is at the Martian surface. Despite the thin air, Martian winds can blow up to 60 miles per hour, stirring up dust that powers enormous dust storms and vast fields of alien sand dunes. Once upon a time, however, wind and water moved across the red planet. Robotic rovers have uncovered clear evidence that billions of years ago, lakes and rivers of liquid water flowed across the red planet's surface. This implies that at some point in the far-off past, Mars's atmosphere was sufficiently thick and retained enough heat for water to stay liquid on the red planet's surface. Not so today: although water ice is plentiful beneath the Martian surface and in its polar ice caps, there are no large bodies of liquid water on the surface there today. Mars also lacks an active plate tectonic system, the geologic motor that drives our dynamic Earth, and is also missing a planetary magnetic field. The absence of this protective shield makes it simpler for the sun's high-energy particles to strip away the red planet's atmosphere, which may help clarify why Mars's atmosphere is now so thin. But in the ancient past, Mars appears to have had an inner dynamo driving a planet-wide magnetic field. What turned off the Martian dynamo? Scientists are still working to determine.1e:[\"$\",\"li\",\"454f334f-c5fb-449a-9789-f72c91cebce4\",{\"id\":\"item-7\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"article\",null,{\"className\":\"border-line bg-white p-[22px] shadow-card rounded-lg border\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2.5\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"aria-hidden\":true,\"className\":\"font-heading flex shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-ink-strong font-bold text-white