e—mostly carbon dioxide, nitrogen, and argon, with surface pressure some 50 times lower than atop Mount Everest—now drives 60-mph winds that fuel dust storms, yet rovers show billions of years ago a thicker, warmer atmosphere let liquid water carve lakes and rivers before Mars, lacking plate tectonics and a magnetic field, lost much air to solar particles, leaving scientists unsure what shut down its ancient protective dynamo.\"}]]]}]]}]]}]}]],\"$L34\"]}]}]\n35:T56e,The history of cartography is a story of human ingenuity and the desire to understand our place in the world. Early maps were often symbolic representations, mixing geographical knowledge with mythology and religious beliefs. The ancient Greeks, particularly Ptolemy, made significant strides by introducing concepts of latitude and longitude, creating a grid system that allowed for more accurate positioning. However, it was not until the Age of Exploration that mapmaking underwent a dramatic transformation. Explorers returning from voyages to the Americas, Africa, and Asia brought back detailed coastlines and new lands, forcing cartographers to constantly update their charts. A major breakthrough came in 1569 when Gerardus Mercator created a world map using a new projection. This cylindrical map projection was revolutionary because it preserved local angles and shapes, making it invaluable for nautical navigation. Sailors could plot straight lines of constant bearing, known as rhumb lines, which greatly simplified long-distance sea travel. Despite its distortion of area at high latitudes, making Greenland appear much larger than Africa, the Mercator projection became the standard for navigation and remains widely used in classrooms and atlases today, illustrating how a practical solution to a navigational problem can have a lasting impact on how we visualize the world.1f:[\"$\",\"li\",\"230b4c42-4aea-590f-a175-308a8d3e8e74\",{\"id\":\"item-8\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"article\",null,{\"className\":\"border-l